        "Section 2's penalty has never been enforced: Congress never reduced a state's House seats, even while Southern states kept most Black citizens from voting for decades. Its words 'or other crime' are why states may bar people with felony convictions from voting, as the Court held in Richardson v. Ramirez (1974).",
        "Section 3 returned to the news after January 6, 2021. In 2022 a New Mexico judge removed a county commissioner from office under Section 3, a provision rarely enforced since the years after the Civil War. But in Trump v. Anderson (2024), the Supreme Court held that states cannot enforce Section 3 against candidates for federal office; that power belongs to Congress."
